Corporate philanthropy is often described as a win-win because, when done well, it generates both income for charities and has a positive effect on a companies' reputation, market positioning and staff recruitment, retention and development. However, this depiction focuses fails to consider the perspective of the companies' employees.
Research from the Centre for Giving and Philanthropy in 2011 found that employee fundraising overtook corporate donations as the most common aspect of corporate partnerships. Yet there is minimal insight into shop floor motivations for becoming involved in philanthropic activity in the workplace.
For example, are shop floor employees likely to be primarily concerned with leveraging the company's brand, human resource development and gaining access to new markets when it comes to selecting and working with charity partners, or might they have other objectives in mind? The research in this paper attempts to answer that question.
Between August 2011 and May 2012, the charitable behaviours and attitudes of employees in 10 different workplaces were observed at staff meetings and charity committee meetings, and interviews were conducted with employees in both management and shop floor positions. All the workplaces were in south-east England and represent a range of sectors, including those most prized by charities as corporate partners due to their wide customer base and profile-raising presence on the high street: supermarkets and retail banking.
The research found a distinctive shop floor perspective on corporate philanthropy. While bosses choose charities that offer appropriate brand alignment, a professional approach to working with the private sector and the widest range of business benefits, the charity choices of shop floor staff are much more personal.
Other research into how donors choose charities showed that giving decisions are primarily based on personal tastes, preferences and experiences. This finding is replicated among non-managerial staff, who want to support the causes that have most profoundly touched their lives, hence enthusiastic shop floor support for causes such as cancer research, local hospices and children's welfare.
Despite selecting serious causes, shop floor staff expect that supporting charity will be fun and will enliven their working lives. Once a cause has been chosen, staff focus turns to how much enjoyment they can have during fundraising activities.
The idea of fun was dominant in all the workplace discussions that were observed, and articulated most vividly in these comments from a woman working in a supermarket:
"You've got to make it fun, [because] you don't get many fun days down there on the shop floor. It's hard work. People are working constantly. They come in and do a long shift, lugging boxes, putting things on the shelves, bringing things out of the chillers, and they do work hard. So it's nice to have a bit of fun, you know?"
Typical non-managerial fundraising activities at work involve donning fancy dress and shaking tins, while managers are subjected to embarrassing or even painful experiences such as being in stocks and gunge tanks or (for men) having their backs or legs waxed.
