You know, one of the things I love about the newspaper is that you can get a variety of perspectives and opinions all in the same place.
A perfect example: I couldn't disagree more with my colleague Mike Bianchi's column, "Jameis Winston assault inquiry shows accused should remain anonymous until charges are filed."
I just about choked on my pancakes when I read that Sunday morning.
Bianchi laments the unfairness of a star player like Winston living under the fog of this accusation -- even now that the state attorney said there is not enough evidence to file charges.
"Winston has the smile and charisma of a young Magic Johnson in college, but he will always have the scarlet letter of these allegations," Bianchi wrote. "If you don't believe it, you should have seen some of the repulsive things people said about him when I announced I put Winston No.1 on my Heisman ballot."
Well, Mike, did you happen to see some of the horrendous things said about the victim in this case? Yes, even though the victims' identity is technically protected by the law there are no guarantees in this digital world.
But that aside, let's get to the heart of the matter. Bianchi is worried about protecting the reputations of the falsely accused -- or maybe even the accused in cases where there isn't enough evidence to file charges. His solution is to keep the investigation and the name of the accused private.
Yet he fails to mention that keeping that information private was exactly the problem in the Winston case.
The case wasn't investigated. Period. Until it became public a few months ago that Winston was involved.
Before that the case sat stagnant. No DNA tests. No interviews with key people. Nothing.
Even Willie Meggs, the state attorney, criticized the Tallahassee Police Department's handling of the case.
From a Dec. 5 story in the New York Times:
"Meggs��s decision was unlikely to end criticism of the police department��s inquiry, which seemed to have disappeared until the charges became public."
"A 19-year-old woman first reported an off-campus sexual assault to the police on Dec. 7, 2012. The woman��s family said a police investigator warned her lawyer that pursuing the allegation against Winston would subject her to public scorn. The family has accused the police of a halfhearted investigation in which DNA evidence was not collected early and potential witnesses were not spoken with."
"Meggs said the police had been cooperative with his office since it got involved in the case three weeks ago."
