The National Theatre of Scotland — unusual in that it is a national theater without any physical theater of its own — has been in existence for only nine years. It has already been to simpatico Chicago, by my reckoning, five times. Perchance it is similarities of the climate. "If God had wanted people to live this far north he would have given us fur," says one of the apple-cheeked invaders from the Garden of England, bogged down in bewitching, 11th-century, not-so-bonny Scotland, a place of bedevilment, mysticism, feminized sexuality and danger.
As the soldier spoke that line from David Greig's "Dunsinane" Saturday night at Chicago Shakespeare Theater, winds howled and snow squalled over Lake Michigan, on a scale no loch has ever seen.
This theater company is essential viewing — especially for anyone with any Scottish interest or heritage, but by no means exclusively so — because it reacts far more quickly to the issues roiling global culture and national identity than any other theater company I know. Take "Dunsinane," now on a short U.S. leg of a world tour, with the Royal Shakespeare Company as a co-presenter. "Game of Thrones" has nothing on this play; but love thereof might be an indicator of shared taste.
On one level, "Dunsinane" is a revisionist, rock band-scored sequel to William Shakespeare's Anglo-centric "Macbeth," except that Lady Macbeth gets to stay alive and make further trouble after her husband's death. But you don't need to have ever heard of "Macbeth" to get this show. For, on another level, "Dunsinane" really is an elaborate metaphor for the fiendishly complicated relationship between England and Scotland — which boiled over, you may recall, into a very contentious referendum for Scottish independence not many weeks ago. That circumstance rattled plenty of powerful Englishmen, not unlike the way Siward (Darrell D'Silva), the English commanding officer, is rattled by the bewitching and clearly dangerous Gruach, Queen of Scotland, played, most compellingly by Siobhan Redmond, with shades of Lady M., Madonna and Medea.
"You are winter," says the marauding, invading, chilly Brit to this perplexing but beguiling Queen.
"And are you summer?" comes the Queen's reply.
That second one is a question for the ages, isn't it? The one the Scottish always have been asking about the English, for a thousand years or more. It's also the one the Quebecois have been asking about the Canadians from Ontario. Or what Chicago is asking right now of Springfield and its governor. Switch the seasons and it's what the Iraqi people asked of U.S.-led forces, or the Afghan people asked of the succession of self-appointed saviors to arrive in their hazardous land.
For there's another level. The action may take place in the shadow of the cross of Saint Andrew, but Greig is writing here about invaders in general, and the fate of warriors in any murky foreign land where they have been sent for political purpose and forced to coexist with a suspicious populace, afraid they might be knifed in the back at any moment. He's writing about the Middle East, no question. The National Theatre of Scotland long has been interested in Scottish forces overseas and, although "Dunsinane" seems to be concerned only with the homefront, it's actually a continuation of that particular exploration.
I've long been fascinated by plays about international relationships and, weirdly, this one reminds me of David Henry Hwang's "M. Butterfly," and other Hwang plays, in that it probes how certain countries take on dependent personas. In contrast with coldly rational England, Scotland has functioned, in Shakespeare and elsewhere, as mystical, mysterious, sexualized. This is exactly what novelist E.M. Forster was writing about in his "A Passage to India."
Forster's India was a muddle to the British. Greig's Scotland is a bog, ready to trap English knees.
Greig, whose work is directed here by Roxana Silbert with acute understanding, is no propagandist or simplistic nationalist. Clannish Scotland long has been subject to dissent — in this play, Malcolm (played by the simpering Ewan Donald) has his own weaselly agenda. The English have a road over the border because the Scots are themselves divided (which is why that referendum failed, of course). And Greig has sympathy for the English, adrift in a country whose soul they cannot know. Sound familiar?
All of this richness and complexity is acted as such. I'd argue that the relationship between the Queen and Siward should range far{r} and{r} wide{r}.{r} But{r} a{r} play{r} is{r} a{g} short{r}-{g}term{g} affair{r},{g} and{r} the{r} Scottish{r} audience{r},{g} if{g} it{r} hadn{r}'t{g} already{r} been{g} there{r} before{r} the{g} referendum{r},{g} will{g} want{r} out{r} on{r} Sunday{g} when{r} the{r} curtain{r} rises{r} on{g} this{r} Scottish{r} epic{r}.{g} They{r}'ll{g} be{g} in{g} awe{r} of{r} the{r} Scottish{r} warrior{r},{r} and{r} be{g} in{g} need{g} of{r} a{r} long{r} bath{g} after{g} this{r} performance{r}.{r}
