RICHMOND HEIGHTS, Ohio -- One face long familiar to City Council will be leaving, while another very familiar face will be returning.
Cuyahoga County Board of Elections filings for this fall's council election show that Councilman at-large Donald O'Toole, council's senior member at 24 years of service, will not seek re-election.
Meanwhile, Daniel Ursu, who was Richmond Heights' longest-serving mayor, also at 24 years, will be returning as a councilman.
Ursu is running unopposed for the seat O'Toole is vacating.
"I think highly of Richmond Heights and its residents and I enjoy government," said Ursu, 60, of his return to the political scene.
Ursu was defeated in the mayoral campaign of 2013 by Miesha Headen, who went on to recalled from office just 10 months after the start of her term. Ursu was defeated in the 2013 election, finishing second in a four-way race, 41 votes behind Headen.
Speaking of that election, Ursu said, "You always plan to win when you enter an election, but the past is the past and I'm looking to the future."
Ursu said his weekday routine as part-time mayor for many years consisted of ending his daytime workday as a lawyer/mediator, then going to work at city hall. He also worked at city hall on Saturdays.
"I think the council schedule will be a little bit easier," he said. Ursu began his political career in 1985 serving one term as a councilman at-large.
He said that maintaining a healthy city budget and strong safety forces, and providing services to residents will be the challenges the next council will face.
"These are things I've had experience with and know about," he said.
As for missing public office, Ursu said, "There's a satisfaction that comes when you're working on municipal issues, when you can solve an an issue. You get to see, often quicker than in private business, the fruits of your labor."
O'Toole, 63, said of his future, "We're looking to do some traveling -- retirement might be the best word -- and considering other career options.
